<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Heath</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Heath was a British Conservative politician and prime minister of the United Kingdom from 1970 to 1974. His government took the country into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a> in 1973 but struggled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> and industrial conflict, and a miners' strike and the three-day week led to his defeat in 1974; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> replaced him as party leader in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-heath.jpg" alt="Edward Heath" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan Warren · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Heath_4_Allan_Warren.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
